WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.060
SPEAKER 1: Google Cloud comes
with built-in infrastructure

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:08.410
and tools to help you develop
apps and manage your services.

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:11.910
In this episode, we'll go
beyond login and error reporting

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:15.390
and explore the powers
of tracing, profiling,

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:17.400
and debugging in production.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:17.970
Stick around.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:21.330
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.220
Google Cloud Log
tools are great.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:30.070
And I hope you didn't miss the
previous episodes about Cloud

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:33.070
Logging and error reporting.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:35.830
But when dealing with
logs, it's easy to get

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:39.700
lost looking for needle
in a haystack, especially

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.400
when you're trying to determine
where time is actually

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.360
spent when your application is
carrying out a particular task.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:49.000
Cloud Trace is a
distributed tracing system

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.460
to help you with
this exact problem

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.640
by collecting latency data
across multiple Google Cloud

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.910
products and many
supported languages.

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:01.180
In most cases, Cloud
Trace requires no setup.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.540
The cloud tracing graphical
interface in the console

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.080
provides recent
traces, each with URI,

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.400
latency, and timestamp,
but also most frequent URIs

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.330
and even insights pointing you
to common application problems.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:19.120
For any given request
URI, a heat map

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.360
of request duration through
time is presented, giving you

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.420
visual hints about which
specific requests may

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.730
need further investigation.

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:30.460
Capturing distributed
traces from your application

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:33.070
used to be a difficult
problem, but we now

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:36.730
make it easy with OpenTelemetry,
previously named OpenCensus,

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:39.580
a project that we
started here at Google.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:42.280
For any running code
that isn't integrated out

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.250
of the box with Cloud
Trace, OpenTelemetry

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:49.720
provides libraries that capture
traces and application metrics

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.750
from your applications
either automatically, using

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:57.260
included instrumentation,
or manually, via the API.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.590
Once you've identified
a particular bottleneck

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:02.260
in your application that
is likely to require a code

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:06.280
modification, it's
time to use a profiler.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.680
What's special
about Cloud Profiler

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:11.290
is the little to no
overhead that it introduces

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:15.070
while continuously gathering
CPU and memory allocation

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:18.010
data from your
production applications.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:20.710
Cloud Profiler then attributes
that information back

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:23.170
to the application
source code, helping

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:26.320
you identify what's
consuming the most resources

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:29.080
and how to fix your
performance issues.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.240
Once collected,
profiling data is

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:33.640
displayed using
flame graphs, which

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:35.530
pack a large amount
of information

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:38.800
into a compact and
readable format.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.630
This is not the time
to go into a course

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:43.450
on troubleshooting your
code, but I would instead

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:46.450
recommend that you interact
with the Cloud Profiler user

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:47.990
interface.

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:51.070
Click on the Top Functions
list, and find out

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.470
which are the most
expensive functions,

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:56.560
and then click on one to
enable the focused view.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.300
There may well be
some low-hanging fruit

00:02:58.300 --> 00:02:59.800
waiting for you.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:02.740
Writing code is great,
but potentially,

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:07.720
finding hard to identify
bugs is just as important.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:11.320
Now, be honest-- how often
have you had to restart

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:14.830
or redeploy your application
just because you added a log

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:16.660
statement for debugging?

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:18.220
I know I have.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:20.290
Cloud Debugger is
a tool you can use

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:23.260
to inspect the state of
an application at any code

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:27.400
location without stopping or
slowing down the running app.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:30.730
Since we're talking about
live-running applications here,

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:33.220
there are no actual
breakpoints, but instead

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:38.140
two unique features--
snapshots and logpoints.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:42.010
With snapshots, you can capture
the call stack and inspect

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:45.460
local variables, all without
stopping or slowing down

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:46.600
the app.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:50.230
With logpoints, you can
inject logging statements

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:52.390
without the need
to restart the app.

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:55.440
Creating a logpoint a lot
like creating a snapshot.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.970
Simply click on a given
line in your code,

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:01.720
and each logpoint will operate
just as a log statement

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:04.210
that you write into
your code manually.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:07.300
You can add free text,
reference variables,

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:10.300
and set the conditions
for the log to be saved.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.000
Logpoints are written
to your standard output,

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.510
which means that you can use
them with any logging back end.

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:18.410
This is no conventional
debugging tool.

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:20.769
It's one that can
help you troubleshoot

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:22.750
hard-to-reproduce problems.

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:26.140
So I can only strongly
encourage you to look into it.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:30.730
With Cloud Trace, Profiler,
and Debugger in your tool box,

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:34.420
you now have modern and powerful
tools to diagnose and fix

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:38.790
issues that may arise in your
cloud apps, even in production.

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:42.220
If you like this video, please
like, subscribe, comment,

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:45.690
share, and look forward to more
Google Cloud Essentials videos.

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:49.640
